name: sed
boss: Lookaround
flavor: I remember what you wrote. I match it again.
brief: sed edits a stream. It reads a line, applies your commands, prints the
brief: result. The one you will use forever is s/old/new/ -- and the trailing
brief: g, which changes every match on the line instead of only the first.
brief:
brief: -n turns off the automatic printing, which turns sed into a selector:
brief: -n '5p' prints only line 5. -i edits the file in place, and on a
brief: read-only root that only works on files you put somewhere writable.
---
q: {replace the first @old@ on each line of @cfg@ with @new@|swap the first @old@ for @new@ on every line of @cfg@|change the first occurrence of @old@ per line to @new@ in @cfg@}
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/' @cfg@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: s/pattern/replacement/ -- no trailing g means only the first match per line
---
q: {replace EVERY @old@ on every line of @cfg@ with @new@|replace every occurrence of @old@ with @new@ throughout @cfg@|swap every @old@ for @new@ on every line of @cfg@}
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/g' @cfg@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: the g goes after the final slash
---
q: {replace @old@ with @new@ in @cfg@, ignoring case|replace @old@ with @new@ in @cfg@, matching case-insensitively|swap @old@ for @new@ in @cfg@ without caring about case}
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/gi' @cfg@
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/Ig' @cfg@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: i (or I) after the slashes makes the match case-insensitive
---
q: %show% only line 3 of @log@, and nothing else
a: sed -n '3p' @log@
out: WARN
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: -n suppresses the automatic print, so only what you p yourself appears
---
q: %show% lines 2 through 4 of @log@
a: sed -n '2,4p' @log@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: same p as line 3, but a range with a comma before it
---
q: %show% every line of @log@ that mentions @sev@, using sed rather than grep
a: sed -n '/@sev@/p' @log@
out: @sev@
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: a pattern in slashes acts as the address, selecting lines that match
---
q: {delete every line of @log@ mentioning @sev@|remove any line of @log@ that mentions @sev@|drop every @sev@ line from @log@}
a: sed '/@sev@/d' @log@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: d deletes the whole line the pattern matched, not just the text
---
q: {delete the comment lines -- the ones starting with # -- from @cfg@|remove every line of @cfg@ that starts with #|drop the # comment lines out of @cfg@}
a: sed '/^#/d' @cfg@
out: listen
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: ^# anchors the match to the start of the line
---
q: {delete the blank lines from @cfg@|remove every empty line from @cfg@|drop the blank lines out of @cfg@}
a: sed '/^$/d' @cfg@
out: timeout
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: ^$ matches a line with nothing between start and end
---
q: {delete the first line of @csv@, the header row|drop the header row from @csv@|remove line 1 -- the header -- from @csv@}
a: sed '1d' @csv@
out: ^gateway
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: a bare line number with d deletes just that line
---
q: {strip every leading space from the lines of @log@|remove the leading spaces from each line of @log@|delete whatever spaces open each line of @log@}
a: sed 's/^ *//' @log@
out: 2024
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: ^ *  matches leading spaces, replaced with nothing
---
q: {do two edits in one pass -- drop comments, then drop blank lines -- from @cfg@|drop comments and blank lines from @cfg@ in a single sed invocation|in one pass, remove both comments and blank lines from @cfg@}
a: sed -e '/^#/d' -e '/^$/d' @cfg@
out: listen
ref: sed
teach: sed -e
hint: -e lets you give more than one script
---
q: {pull the number out of every port line in @cfg@ using an extended regex|extract the number from each port line in @cfg@ with an extended regex|using extended regex, pull just the number out of every port line in @cfg@}
a: sed -E 's/^port = ([0-9]+)$/\1/' @cfg@
out: 8080
ref: sed
teach: sed -E
hint: -E gives you (groups) without backslashes; \1 is what the group caught
---
q: {same thing, but with the older flag letter for extended regular expressions|repeat the port-number extraction using the older extended-regex flag letter|do the same port-number pull, but with the legacy extended-regex flag}
a: sed -r 's/^port = ([0-9]+)$/\1/' @cfg@
out: 8080
ref: sed
teach: sed -r
hint: -r and -E are the same flag; -E is the portable spelling
---
q: {swap the first two comma-separated fields of @csv@|reverse the order of the first two comma-separated fields in @csv@|exchange @csv@'s first two comma-separated fields}
a: sed -E 's/^([^,]+),([^,]+)/\2,\1/' @csv@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed -E
hint: capture each field in a group, then reorder them with \1 and \2
---
q: {copy @cfg@ to edit.ini and then change @old@ to @new@ inside it, in place|copy @cfg@ to edit.ini, then edit edit.ini in place changing @old@ to @new@|make a copy of @cfg@ named edit.ini and swap @old@ for @new@ in it directly}
a: cp @cfg@ edit.ini && sed -i 's/@old@/@new@/g' edit.ini
state: exists:edit.ini
ref: sed
teach: sed -i
hint: -i writes the file back rather than printing it
---
q: %show% only the last line of @log@ using sed
a: sed -n '$p' @log@
out: out of memory
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: $ means "the last line"
---
q: %show% every line of @log@ from the first @sev@ to the end
a: sed -n '/@sev@/,$p' @log@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: a pattern address and $ together mean from the first match to the end
---
q: {run a sed script held in a file rather than typed on the command line|run sed using a script file instead of an inline expression|apply a sed script that's stored in a file, not typed inline}
a: printf 's/@old@/@new@/g\n' > s.sed && sed -f s.sed @cfg@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed -f
hint: -f takes a filename holding the script, like -e takes it inline
---
q: {edit a copy in place, keeping the original as edit.ini.bak|edit edit.ini in place, but back up the original as edit.ini.bak first|make the same in-place edit, saving the pre-edit version as edit.ini.bak}
a: cp @cfg@ edit.ini && sed -i.bak 's/@old@/@new@/g' edit.ini
state: exists:edit.ini.bak
ref: sed
teach: sed -i[SFX]
hint: a suffix stuck to -i means "back the original up under this name"
